11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L18",null,{"guide":{"date":"February–August 1861","place":"Texas and southern New Mexico Territory, from San Antonio to Fort Bliss, Mesilla, and Fort Fillmore","result":"Texas authorities took control of U.S. Army posts and property after David Twiggs surrendered the Department of Texas. John R. Baylor then marched west with the 2nd Texas Mounted Rifles, occupied Mesilla, and forced Major Isaac Lynde to evacuate Fort Fillmore. Baylor captured more than 500 U.S. soldiers near San Augustine Springs on July 27, 1861, and proclaimed a Confederate Territory of Arizona south of the 34th parallel on August 1","facts":[{"label":"Texas secession ordinance","value":"February 1, 1861"},{"label":"Texas referendum","value":"February 23: 46,100–14,600 for secession"},{"label":"U.S. property surrendered in Texas","value":"February 18, 1861"},{"label":"Baylor entered New Mexico","value":"July 23, 1861"},{"label":"Fort Fillmore evacuated","value":"1 a.m., July 27"},{"label":"Forces stated in episode","value":"Fewer than 300 Texans captured more than 500 U.S. troops"},{"label":"Confederate Arizona proclaimed","value":"August 1, 1861"}],"maps":[{"title":"The campaign area today","caption":"This episode moves from San Antonio across West Texas to El Paso, Mesilla, and Fort Fillmore in the Rio Grande valley. The later campaign continued north toward Santa Fe.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-107.5%2C28.0%2C-97.0%2C36.5&layer=mapnik&marker=32.2701%2C-106.8008"},{"title":"Baylor’s advance and the Fort Fillmore surrender","caption":"Red shows Baylor’s route west and his movement into Mesilla. Blue shows Lynde’s unsuccessful attack and the attempted desert retreat toward Fort Stanton before the U.S. column surrendered.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/elca/learn/historyculture/the-civil-war.htm","image":"/images/baylor-new-mexico-1861-map.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Baylor’s march from San Antonio to Mesilla and the evacuation and surrender of the Fort Fillmore garrison"},{"title":"Texas and New Mexico before the campaign","caption":"An 1857 U.S. Army map showing settlements, rivers, routes, and military posts across Texas, New Mexico Territory, and northern Mexico.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/g4030.fi000103/","image":"/images/texas-new-mexico-1857-map.jpg","alt":"1857 U.S. Army map of Texas and part of New Mexico showing roads, rivers, towns, and military posts"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:19","audioTime":79,"title":"The New Mexico campaign extended the war far west","detail":"The 1861–62 Confederate invasion of New Mexico was the westernmost major campaign of the Civil War and included two major battles and several smaller engagements."},{"time":"2:09","audioTime":129,"title":"Davis authorized Sibley to organize a brigade","detail":"Henry Hopkins Sibley resigned his U.S. Army commission, presented a plan to Jefferson Davis, and received authority on July 5, 1861, to raise mounted volunteers in Texas and drive U.S. forces from New Mexico."},{"time":"3:25","audioTime":205,"title":"Sibley planned a wider western advance","detail":"According to his artillery chief, Sibley intended to take New Mexico, move into Colorado’s mining districts, and eventually reach ports in Southern California."},{"time":"4:10","audioTime":250,"title":"Kit Carson entered U.S. service","detail":"Carson became lieutenant colonel of the 1st New Mexico Volunteer Infantry. The episode uses him to introduce local opposition to the Confederate invasion."},{"time":"7:12","audioTime":432,"title":"New Mexico Territory covered a much larger area","detail":"In 1860 the territory included present-day New Mexico and Arizona and the southern tip of Nevada, with about 100,000 residents."},{"time":"7:58","audioTime":478,"title":"Roads and the Rio Grande controlled movement","detail":"The Santa Fe Trail entered from the northeast, the Overland Mail route crossed southern New Mexico, and the Camino Real followed the Rio Grande. The territory had no railroad or telegraph."},{"time":"12:42","audioTime":762,"title":"Texas secessionists bypassed Sam Houston","detail":"Delegates elected without effective Unionist opposition met at Austin and adopted a secession ordinance on February 1, 1861, by 166 votes to 8."},{"time":"14:23","audioTime":863,"title":"A committee began seizing U.S. property","detail":"Before the public referendum, the convention created a Committee of Public Safety and authorized it to take federal posts, supplies, and other property in Texas."},{"time":"16:06","audioTime":966,"title":"Texas demanded the surrender of the Department of Texas","detail":"The committee sent commissioners to David Twiggs while Ben McCulloch brought about 1,000 armed men into San Antonio to support the demand."},{"time":"18:14","audioTime":1094,"title":"Twiggs surrendered U.S. posts and supplies","detail":"On February 18, Twiggs agreed to turn over federal buildings, military property, and 19 forts and posts. Roughly 2,500 U.S. soldiers were ordered to leave Texas by the Gulf Coast."},{"time":"20:08","audioTime":1208,"title":"Texas voters approved secession","detail":"The February 23 referendum passed 46,100 to 14,600. Sam Houston refused to swear allegiance to the Confederacy and was removed as governor in March."},{"time":"21:28","audioTime":1288,"title":"Texas formed mounted regiments for its frontier","detail":"John R. Baylor became lieutenant colonel of the 2nd Texas Mounted Rifles and was sent west to secure abandoned U.S. posts, including Fort Bliss."},{"time":"24:47","audioTime":1487,"title":"Van Dorn ordered Baylor toward Fort Fillmore","detail":"Earl Van Dorn sent Baylor from San Antonio to West Texas and authorized an attack on Fort Fillmore, about 40 miles north of Fort Bliss."},{"time":"25:51","audioTime":1551,"title":"The march reduced Baylor’s force","detail":"About 1,000 men left San Antonio, but desertion, illness, heat, fatigue, and detachments at posts left roughly 400 at El Paso in early July."},{"time":"26:53","audioTime":1613,"title":"Baylor invaded New Mexico with fewer than 300 men","detail":"He moved north on July 23. After a deserter warned Fort Fillmore, Baylor crossed the Rio Grande and entered Mesilla."},{"time":"27:39","audioTime":1659,"title":"Lynde failed to remove Baylor from Mesilla","detail":"Major Isaac Lynde brought most of his larger garrison across the river, made an unsuccessful attack on July 25, and returned to Fort Fillmore."},{"time":"29:04","audioTime":1744,"title":"The U.S. garrison evacuated Fort Fillmore","detail":"Lynde left at 1 a.m. on July 27 and took the desert road toward Fort Stanton. Heat, lack of water, and whiskey taken from the post weakened and scattered the column."},{"time":"30:08","audioTime":1808,"title":"Lynde surrendered near San Augustine Springs","detail":"Only about 100 soldiers formed when Baylor caught the column. Lynde surrendered more than 500 men to fewer than 300 Texans without a final exchange of fire."},{"time":"31:07","audioTime":1867,"title":"Baylor proclaimed Confederate Arizona","detail":"Unable to continue north with his small force, Baylor remained at Mesilla. On August 1 he declared the area south of the 34th parallel the Confederate Territory of Arizona and named himself governor."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"David E. Twiggs","command":"Department of Texas","subordinates":"Surrendered the department’s posts and property to Texas authorities"},{"commander":"Isaac Lynde","command":"Fort Fillmore garrison","subordinates":"About 700 regulars before detachments; evacuated and surrendered the column"},{"commander":"Edward R. S. Canby","command":"Department of New Mexico","subordinates":"Organized the defense of the territory after the loss of Fort Fillmore"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Earl Van Dorn","command":"Department of Texas","subordinates":"Ordered Baylor to secure West Texas and authorized action against Fort Fillmore"},{"commander":"Rip Ford","command":"2nd Texas Mounted Rifles","subordinates":"Regimental commander; guarded the lower Rio Grande with part of the regiment"},{"commander":"John R. Baylor","command":"Battalion, 2nd Texas Mounted Rifles","subordinates":"Occupied Mesilla, captured Lynde’s force, and proclaimed Confederate Arizona"},{"commander":"Henry H. Sibley","command":"Army of New Mexico being organized","subordinates":"Raised a larger Texas brigade for the next phase of the invasion"}]}],"people":[{"name":"John R. Baylor","role":"Led the first Confederate invasion of New Mexico, captured the Fort Fillmore garrison, and proclaimed Confederate Arizona","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Isaac Lynde","role":"Commanded Fort Fillmore and surrendered more than 500 U.S. soldiers after the desert retreat","side":"United States"},{"name":"David E. Twiggs","role":"Surrendered the U.S. Army’s Department of Texas to state authorities","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/david-twiggs.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670405/"},{"name":"Henry H. Sibley","role":"Proposed the New Mexico invasion and organized the larger force that would follow Baylor","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Christopher “Kit” Carson","role":"Joined the 1st New Mexico Volunteer Infantry as lieutenant colonel","side":"United States","image":"/images/kit-carson.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017894063/"},{"name":"Sam Houston","role":"Opposed Texas secession and was removed as governor after refusing a Confederate loyalty oath","side":"United States"},{"name":"Ben McCulloch","role":"Led armed Texans into San Antonio while commissioners demanded the federal surrender","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/david-twiggs.jpg","alt":"Portrait of David E. Twiggs","caption":"David E. Twiggs commanded the U.S. Army’s Department of Texas and surrendered its posts and property in February 1861.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670405/"},{"image":"/images/kit-carson.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Christopher Kit Carson","caption":"Kit Carson became lieutenant colonel of the 1st New Mexico Volunteer Infantry and later fought in the campaign.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017894063/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 96","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/96-sibleys-new-mexico-campaign-part-the-first"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Civil War on El Camino Real","url":"https://www.nps.gov/elca/learn/historyculture/the-civil-war.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Mesilla historic-site documentation","url":"https://www.nps.gov/elca/learn/management/upload/Comprehensive-Management-Plan-Appendixes-E-and-F-508.pdf"},{"label":"National Park Service: 7th U.S. Infantry service record","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battle-units-detail.htm?battleUnitCode=UUR0007RI"},{"label":"National Park Service: Glorieta Pass campaign overview","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-battle-of-glorieta-pass-a-shattered-dream-teaching-with-historic-places.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: 1857 map of Texas and New Mexico","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/g4030.fi000103/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: David E. Twiggs portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670405/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Kit Carson portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017894063/"}]}}]}]
